The primary aim of this review is to provide a comprehensive and critical evaluation of recent studies addressing
the presence of toxic metal(loid)s in breast milk, their transmission pathways, and their potential toxicological effects on
lactating women and infants.

growth and development (Ballard and Morrow, 2013; Ghane
et al., 2022). The World Health Organization (WHO) recommends exclusive breastfeeding for the first six months of life,
followed by continued breastfeeding up to 2 years of age
alongside nutritionally adequate complementary foods (Syeda
et al., 2021; Hörnell and Lagström, 2024). Although breastfeeding is essential for infant nutrition and development, the
transfer of toxic metal(loid)s (Hg, As, Cr, Cd, and Pb)
through breast milk may pose potential health risks and
increase infants’ vulnerability to toxic effects (Mohammadi
et al., 2022). These toxic metal(loid)s are ranked among the
top 10 chemicals of public health concern by the WHO (WHO
Report, 2020). Infants are particularly susceptible to
metal(loid) toxicity because of their rapid growth, immature
organ systems, and heightened vulnerability of their developing nervous system during the first year of life (Ettinger
et al., 2014). Exposure to metal(loid)s through breast milk
has been associated with adverse health outcomes, including
impaired cognitive and motor development and weakened
immune function. Proper development and maintenance of
infant’s defense, regulatory, and other vital biological systems
require adequate nutritional support, among which optimum
mineral nutrition plays a crucial role (Zwolak et al., 2012).
For example, selenium (Se), an essential trace element,
supports cellular defense mechanisms as a key component
of the antioxidant enzyme glutathione peroxidase. Moreover,
the biological interaction of micronutrients with some foreign
substances provides protection to infants (Awua et al., 2019).
Studies have suggested that Se counteracts the toxicity of As
and Cd (Zwolak, 2020). This can be done primarily in two
ways. One, Se can bind with As and Cd, making an insoluble
complex. This complex formation is a toxic metal trapping
mechanism, preventing them from any further damage to
proteins. DNA. Second, Se can increase As methylation,
which converts a more toxic form of As (As III) to less toxic
forms. (Zwolak, 2020). Moreover, there is an emerging mechanism that says that Se activates the Nrf2 (Nuclear factor
erythroid 2-related factor 2) pathway, which can reduce the
As and Cd toxicity (Zwolak, 2020). Breast milk generally
contains adequate levels of essential micronutrients; however,
contamination with metalloids may significantly affect their
concentrations over prolonged periods (Awua et al., 2019).
The concentration of metal(loid)s in breast milk varies
depending on maternal diet, health status, genetic factors,
and exposure to environmental pollutants (Moon et al., 2025).
Regulation of trace elements in milk involves the uptake of
metals by specific transporters in mammary epithelial cells,
followed by their secretion into the alveolar lumen of the
mammary gland (Bressler et al., 2007). Although the mammary gland possesses an effective biological filtering system,
certain hazardous compounds may still be transferred from
the mother to her breast milk depending on environmental
exposure levels (Vieira et al., 2013). Despite breast milk
representing a potential route of exposure toxic metal(loid)s,
it remains the optimal and primary source of nutrition necessary for the rapid and overall development of infants.
Therefore, careful attention to maternal diet and environmental exposure is essential. Quantification of toxic metal(loid)s
