METHODOLOGY
Different databases, including PubMed, Scopus, Web of
Science, Google Scholar, and ScienceDirect, were searched
for articles published between January 2000 and December
2024 to find relevant studies for this review. The search
phrases included various combinations of “breast milk,”
“feeding mothers,” or “lactation” with terms like “metal,”
“metalloid,” “arsenic,” “cadmium,” “lead,” “mercury,” “chromium,” “concentration,” “level,” “exposure,” “risk,” “contamination,” or “infants’ exposure.” Studies published in English
that reported quantitative concentrations of As, Cd, Cr, Pb,
and / or Hg in human breast milk worldwide were included
in this review. Research involving animals, studies with fewer
than ten samples, those lacking quantifiable data, and duplicate publications were excluded. Following title and abstract
screening of 322 records and full-text assessment of eligible
articles, 97 papers were selected for data extraction, including
metal(loid) concentrations, exceedance rates, and infant
exposure risk assessments. Study quality was evaluated based
on sample size and analytical methodologies, with particular
consideration given to studies employing (ICP – MS) or
atomic absorption spectrometry.

