Assessment of Carcinogenic and Non- carcinogenic Health Risks of Metals in the Drinking
Water of Gombe Local Government Area, Nigeria.
Sule S.1*, Olowolafe E. A.2 and Dabi D. D.2
Department of Geography and Environmental Management, Faculty of Sciencex, Gombe State
University, PMB 127 Gombe, Nigeria
2
Department of Geography and Planning, University of Jos, Jos, Plateau State, Nigeria

1

Corresponding Author: ssule@gsu.edu.ng
ABSTRACT
The carcinogenic and non-carcinogenic health risks of heavy metals (Pb, Fe, Cu, Cr, Mn, and Cd)
in drinking water sources in Gombe LGA, Nigeria, were assessed. A total of 87 samples of water
were collected from the tap, boreholes, wells, vendor water, rainwater, sachet water, and bottled
water in the 11 wards of Gombe LGA. The concentrations of heavy metal were analyzed using
atomic absorption spectrophotometry, while the carcinogenic and non-carcinogenic health risks
were determined using Chronic Daily Intake (CDI), Hazard Quotient (HQ), Hazard Index (HI),
and Incremental Lifetime Cancer Risk (ILCR) models in accordance with USEPA-approved
equation. The results indicated an elevated non-carcinogenic risks (HI ≥ 4) in tap, well, and sachet
water for both infants, children, and adults, with Cr and Pb as the major contributors. Borehole,
rain, vendor, and bottled water sources were found to be low to medium risk levels. The
carcinogenic risk assessment showed that the ILCR values for Cr, Pb, and Cd in tap, well, and
sachet water sources, exceeded the acceptable threshold (1 × 10⁻⁴), especially for adults and
children, and chromium is the main cause of lifetime cancer. The study came to the conclusion
that some drinking water sources in Gombe LGA, especially tap, well, and sachet water, pose
serious public health risks. Thus, strict regulatory enforcement, better water treatment, and
ongoing monitoring are constantly needed to lower metal contamination and protect vulnerable
groups.
Keywords: Carcinogenic risk, Drinking water, Gombe, Heavy metals, Non-carcinogenic risk.
