can affects vulnerable groups, such as
children, the elderly, and those with
weakened immune systems (Thalia, 2024).

According to the World Health Organization
(WHO), illnesses linked to contaminated
drinking water cause millions of deaths
annually, making waterborne diseases a
major global public health concern (WHO,
2019). Access to clean and safe drinking
water has a detrimental effect on public
health, economic development, and overall
well-being in many Nigerian communities,
such as Gombe Local Government Area
(LGA), where waterborne illnesses like
cholera, diarrhea, typhoid fever and cancer
are common (Nwinyi et al., 2020;
Muhammad, 2022; Umar et al., 2022; Sule
et al., 2022). The burden of these illnesses

The US Environmental Protection Agency
(US EPA, 2020) highlights the potential
health risks associated with chemical
contamination of groundwater. To evaluate
these risks, the cancer risk and non-cancer
hazard index are calculated for each metal,
distinguishing between carcinogenic and
non-carcinogenic
health
hazards
(Weitekamp et al., 2021). Cancer risk refers
to the likelihood of developing cancer due to
exposure to chemical contaminants in
drinking water (US EPA, 2020). According
to US EPA guidelines, a cancer risk of 1 in 1
20

Bima Journal of Science and Technology, Vol. 10(1A) Mar, 2026 ISSN: 2536-6041

E-ISSN: 3115-4662

DOI: 10.64290/bima.v10i1A.1514

million (1 × 10-6) or lower is generally
considered negligible, while a risk greater
than 1 in 10,000 (1 × 10-4) is typically
deemed unacceptable. Risks falling between
these ranges require careful consideration by
regulators to determine the need for risk
reduction measures.

carcinogenic health risks associated with
them while accounting for daily water
consumption and dermal absorption among
adults, children, and infants.
It is expected that the results would clarify
the metal contamination of Gombe water
sources, providing citizens with important
information to implement preventative
measures and assisting medical experts in
reducing metal pollution. Additionally, the
findings will be used as a standard for
comparisons with other parts of Nigeria and
the world, advancing our knowledge of
metal pollution in water settings.

According to the US EPA (2020), the
process of assessing cancer risk and noncancer hazard index consists of four steps:
gathering data to identify and quantify
pollutants in water samples; calculating
exposure levels while taking frequency and
duration of exposure into account;
determining toxicity through chemical
evaluations; and calculating the non-cancer
hazard index and cancer risk while
accounting for vulnerable groups like
pregnant women and children. Researchers
can calculate the possible health hazards
connected to pollutant exposure by
integrating information from toxicity
evaluations and exposure levels (Owens,
2022).

Study Area
Gombe LGA is located in Gombe state,
North-eastern part of Nigeria. The
geographically, its lies between Latitudes
100 14′10′′N to 100 19′ 00′′N of the Equator;
and Longitudes 110 07′00′′E to 110 12′50′′E
of the Greenwich meridian. It shares borders
with Kwami to the north, Akko to the
southwest, and Yamaltu-Deba to the east
(Figures 1). The climate is characterized by
dry and wet seasons, with an average annual
rainfall of 850mm (Abashiya et al., 2019).
Temperature is between 35-40°C in the
month of March and April while minimum
temperature is recorded during the
harmattan period (Baminda and Dabi, 2011).
The vegetation found in Gombe is
characterized by scattered shrubs and thorn
bushes which fall within the open Sudan
savannah vegetation zone of Nigeria (Mbaya,
2016). Gombe sandstone is the main outcrop
in the study area and its hydrology consists
of numerous seasonal streams (Mbiimbe et
al., 2019).

Numerous prior studies of water quality
have demonstrated that Gombe's rapid
population growth, economic expansion, and
industrial development have threatened the
quality of both surface and underground
water (Edet et al., 2011; Nwinyi et al., 2020;
Umar et al., 2022; Sule et al., 2022). Despite
all of this, no previous study has examined
the presence of heavy metals in drinking
water linked to cancer and non-cancer risks.
This study attempts to close this important
gap by measuring the levels of six heavy
metals (Pb, Fe, Cu, Cr, Mn, and Cd) and
estimating the non-carcinogenic and

